WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:07.040
Let's step away from a database server for just a&nbsp;
moment and talk more about relationships between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:13.520
tables. Relationships should be planned before&nbsp;
a database is ever built. One way to organize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:21.760
relationships is to create an entity relationship&nbsp;
diagram (ERD). An ERD will show, for a database,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:28.320
relationships that should be established or are&nbsp;
established between entities. Some ERDs only show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:34.240
entity names, but most show the columns used in&nbsp;
relationships, as we see with this portion of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:41.680
ERD for the AdventureWorks database. I do not have&nbsp;
all the tables or the columns in each table listed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:49.920
here as this topic's emphasis is simply to learn&nbsp;
what an ERD is and its role in database design.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.800
Note that the primary key field is indicated for&nbsp;
all three of these tables, which is the hallmark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:05.280
of a well-built ERD. Also, we see lines drawn&nbsp;
between columns used in relationships. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.640
this should be built before the actual database&nbsp;
is built so that a database design is clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:18.400
known and can be adjusted if necessary before&nbsp;
the database building process commences.

